.TH CONSULTE 1 "2026-08-06" "bibliothecaire" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
consulte \- file a research request at bibliothecaire's front door
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" bashify: norun
.B consulte
\fB\-\-claim\fR \fITEXT\fR \fB\-\-falsifier\fR \fITEXT\fR \fB\-\-from\fR \fIPROJECT\fR
[\fB\-\-prediction\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-\-context\fR \fITEXT\fR | \fB\-\-context\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B consulte list
[\fB\-\-from\fR \fIPROJECT\fR]
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B consulte show
.I NUMBER
.br
.B consulte
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B consulte
is how any other project asks
.B bibliothecaire
for research. It files a GitHub issue on
.B hf7y/bibliothecaire
labelled
.BR request ,
which is the queue this library's own dispatched run already works.
.PP
It does
.B not
write into bibliothecaire's repository, and that is the whole design rather
than an implementation detail. Filing an issue needs no clone of this library
on the calling host and no push access to any branch of it, so the verb is
callable from every account on every host that has
.BR gh (1)
\- including accounts holding read\-only deploy keys, which is most of them.
Zach, 2026\-08\-06: "they should have issues write permissions. no repo should
need to write into any other anymore."
.SS What it is for
A request is a
.I claim
somebody wants substantiated in the literature, together with
.I what would falsify it.
Both are required. A request naming no falsifier is asking for agreement
rather than for research, and this library refuses to write a flattering story
about the ecosystem; the same rule already governs its briefs, which cannot
ship without a named disanalogy.
.SS The calling convention, and why the label matters
The interface predates this verb. scheduler's registry entry for this project
(\fIschedule/bibliothecaire.conf\fR, 2026\-08\-04) states it in those words \-
"THE INTERFACE IS A GITHUB ISSUE labelled \fBrequest\fR on
hf7y/bibliothecaire. That is the whole calling convention" \- and the batch
prompt that answers the queue reads exactly that label. Requests #6 through
#12 all went through it, every one filed by hand.
.B consulte
mechanizes the hand\-filing. It does not introduce a second convention, and it
files under
.B request
for that reason alone: a request filed under a label nothing reads is a note
in a drawer.
.PP
The labels are the state machine, and they are visible to the caller without
access to this host:
.BR request " -> " in-progress " -> " delivered .
.SS Subcommands
.TP
.B (none)
File a request. This is the default and the main event.
.TP
.B list
Show the queue \- every request, its state and its labels.
.B \-\-from
narrows it to requests
.I this verb
filed for that project. A door you cannot see through is half a door: without
this, a caller who has filed is back to asking a human whether the answer
came.
.TP
.B show \fINUMBER\fR
Print one request in full, with every reply on it. The delivered answer is a
comment; this is how a calling project reads it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-claim \fITEXT\fR
The claim to substantiate. Required. Its first line becomes the issue title,
cut on a word boundary and marked when cut; the full text always goes in the
body, because the title is an index entry and never the record.
.TP
.B \-\-falsifier \fITEXT\fR
What would settle this against you. Required; see DESCRIPTION.
.TP
.B \-\-from \fIPROJECT\fR
The project asking. Required, so the answer has somewhere to go and the queue
can say who is waiting on it.
.TP
.B \-\-prediction \fITEXT\fR
The prediction the claim was tested as, if it was tested. Optional, and
present because a pre\-registered hypothesis carries all three fields \-
claim, prediction, falsifier \- and dropping the middle one would make the
caller paraphrase its own record on the way through the door.
.TP
.B \-\-context \fITEXT\fR
.TQ
.B \-\-context\-file \fIPATH\fR
Anything else the librarian needs: the numbers that came back, the regime, the
commit. A file, because the real case is a results table and shell quoting is
not where a results table should have to survive.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Compose the request and print it; file nothing. Credentials are still checked,
so a dry run that succeeds is evidence the real one would reach GitHub.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
File even though an open request already carries this exact claim. That is the
only refusal this flag lifts.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output, on all three forms.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.SH DUPLICATES
A request whose claim is already open is refused with exit
.BR 9 ,
naming the issue that already carries it. It is not silently deduplicated: a
second call that quietly did nothing and exited 0 would be indistinguishable
from one that filed, and the caller would have no way to tell which happened.
It is refused rather than merged because discovering the duplicate on the
other side of the door costs a real model call.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
.B consulte does not spend money.
Filing is
.B bash
in \fICONTRACT.md\fR's HOW column \- mechanized, free, unattended, no model in
the loop \- so this verb carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag at all, and
.B \-\-help
answers "can this cost me anything?" with no, on its own.
.PP
.B Answering does spend,
and the page names it so the boundary is not mistaken for free.
.B summon
is the HOW of every row under "Answer a filed request" in \fICONTRACT.md\fR: a
model call and a literature search, spent by
.BR bibliothecaire 's
own dispatched run, never by the caller and never at the moment of filing. The
door is free; the work behind it is metered. Filing more requests costs the
filer nothing and costs the library real money, which is why the duplicate
refusal above exists.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Filed, and the issue URL was printed. A dry run that composed a request is
also this.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. A missing
.BR \-\-claim ,
.B \-\-falsifier
or
.BR \-\-from ,
an unreadable
.BR \-\-context\-file ,
a stray argument, and
.B \-\-summon
are each one of these.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it tried to file and could not.
.BR gh (1)
missing, unauthenticated, or refusing the call \- and in every one of those
cases the request was
.B not
filed and the message says so.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read the queue it was asked to report on. This is "I cannot
see", never "nothing to report", because an empty queue printed by a blind run
reads exactly like a queue that is genuinely empty.
.TP
.B 9
This exact claim is already open. Nothing was filed. See DUPLICATES.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONSULTE_REPO
The repository the door opens onto. Defaults to
.BR hf7y/bibliothecaire ,
which is what the verb's name means; the override exists so the test suite can
point at a scratch slug, and is not a user\-facing knob. It is deliberately
.B not
derived from a git remote: an installed verb runs from a build directory with
no repository above it, and one that read the remote would file into whatever
repository the caller's shell happened to be sitting in.
.TP
.B CONSULTE_LABEL
The queue label. Defaults to
.BR request .
Changing it without changing scheduler's
.I BATCH_PROMPT
files requests into a queue nothing reads.
.SH EXAMPLES
Name the utility:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ consulte --version
consulte (bashified)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Authorise spending on a utility that cannot spend. The flag is refused by name
rather than accepted and ignored:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ consulte --summon
consulte: --summon: this utility never spends; the flag does not exist here
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.PP
A request with no falsifier is refused before any network call:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ consulte --from ecosim --claim "decentralisation wins"
consulte: --falsifier is required: name what would settle this against you.
.fi
.RE
.PP
File one, from a project that holds no credential for this repository beyond
.BR gh (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
.\" bashify: norun
$ consulte --claim "$(jq -r '.claim' h5.json)" \\
    --falsifier "$(jq -r '.falsifier' h5.json)" \\
    --prediction "$(jq -r '.prediction' h5.json)" \\
    --context-file run2-results.md \\
    --from ecosim
consulte: filed as hf7y/bibliothecaire#20
  (label: request -- bibliothecaire's research queue)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Then read the answer where it lands:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.\" bashify: norun
$ consulte list --from ecosim
#20   OPEN    request                      research: Hayek's structural claim ...
$ consulte show 20
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I schedule/bibliothecaire.conf
In the
.B scheduler
repository: the registry entry that states this calling convention and carries
the prompt that answers the queue. The label in this utility and the label in
that prompt are the same string by obligation, not by coincidence.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cueille (1),
.BR fonde (1),
.BR range (1),
.BR gh (1),
.BR mail (1)
.PP
Lineage:
.BR mail (1)
in being a front door \- a caller hands over a message and a return address and
walks away, and the work happens on the other side, later, on somebody else's
schedule.
.BR gh (1)
in treating an issue as the transport, so no new bus, daemon or queue file with
its own locking had to be invented for a channel that already exists and that
the human already reads.
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026\-08\-06, from a request that could not be delivered.
.PP
On 2026\-08\-04 ecosim wanted a literature\-grounded second read on two of its
run\-2 hypotheses. It had no way to ask: its own account holds read\-only
deploy keys and no credential for this repository, so the cross\-write shape
then in use \- carry the text into bibliothecaire's
.I briefs/
convention \- was not something that account could do on any day. It wrote a
note asking a human to carry the request,
.BR git\-add (1)\-ed
it on its own clone, and the note sat staged and uncommitted for two days,
visible to nobody. Zach, on reading it: "asking bibliothecaire to do research
is a french\-shaped verb. bibliothecaire needs a front door that ecosim knows
about."
.PP
That request is this verb's first, filed through it rather than about it.
